input.prompt:
Follow-up review on your Context.res fix. The #res branch is correct, but the prepared-headers branch has two robustness problems:

1. Immutability: `else if (_res && this.#preparedHeaders) { this.#mergeCookies(...) }` mutates `_res.headers` WITHOUT first doing `_res = createResponseInstance(_res.body, _res)`. A handler can return a `fetch()` Response (immutable headers) while middleware set a cookie before `next()` (cookie sits in `#preparedHeaders`). `headers.delete('set-cookie')` / `.append` will then throw a TypeError. The setter must create a mutable copy before mutating, in every path that mutates headers.

2. Unnecessary mutation: `#mergeCookies` always deletes and re-appends Set-Cookie whenever `cookies.length > 0`, even when every prepared cookie is already present in `_res` (the normal `c.text()`/`c.json()` path where `#newResponse` already copied them). That is wasted work and triggers problem 1 for ordinary responses. It should no-op when there is nothing new to add.

Please restructure the setter to a single clean flow. Suggested shape (adapt as you see fit, keep style):

```ts
  set res(_res: Response | undefined) {
    if (_res) {
      const prevHeaders = this.#res?.headers
      const cookies = (prevHeaders ?? this.#preparedHeaders)?.getSetCookie() ?? []
      const existingCookies = _res.headers.getSetCookie()
      const missingCookies = cookies.filter((cookie) => !existingCookies.includes(cookie))

      if (prevHeaders || missingCookies.length > 0) {
        _res = createResponseInstance(_res.body, _res)
        if (prevHeaders) {
          for (const [k, v] of prevHeaders.entries()) {
            if (k === 'content-type' || k === 'set-cookie') {
              continue
            }
            _res.headers.set(k, v)
          }
        }
        if (missingCookies.length > 0) {
          _res.headers.delete('set-cookie')
          for (const cookie of [...missingCookies, ...existingCookies]) {
            _res.headers.append('set-cookie', cookie)
          }
        }
      }
    }
    this.#res = _res
    this.finalized = true
  }
```

This keeps: non-cookie #res headers override the incoming response; content-type from the incoming response wins; context cookies first, then existing response cookies; identical cookies deduped; prepared-headers cookies merged but non-cookie prepared headers ignored; a single mutable copy created before any mutation; no mutation when there is nothing to merge. Remove the now-unneeded `#mergeCookies` private method.

Keep your two regression tests and the updated existing test. Add one more test proving immutability safety in the prepared path: use the existing `makeResponseHeaderImmutable` helper pattern, but note that helper only blocks `.set`. To truly cover the delete/append path, extend the local test approach minimally — e.g. wrap the response headers with a Proxy that throws on `set`, `append`, and `delete` when called on the original response, set the cookie via `setCookie(c, ...)` first (prepared branch), then assign; assert it does not throw and both cookies are present. If that is awkward, at minimum add an assertion that the prepared-path assignment does not mutate/throw and produces both cookies; do not weaken existing tests.

Then run:
- `pnpm exec vp test --run src/context.test.ts`
- `pnpm exec vp test --run src/hono.test.ts src/compose.test.ts src/helper/cookie/index.test.ts`
- `pnpm exec vp fmt --check src/context.ts src/context.test.ts`

Leave everything unstaged. Report the final diff, exact test summaries, and confirm no other files changed.

